00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:08.800
KATE FITZPATRICK:
So basically, my job

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:11.380
as an engineer on
UK infrastructure

00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:12.860
is kind of twofold.

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:15.240
First, ensure reliability
for the product,

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:18.070
but also make sure that
it's easy to maintain.

00:02:18.070 --> 00:02:20.800
And on the intersection of
those two different dimensions

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:23.980
is actually the upgrade
story, because as you know,

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:26.590
we're on a fleet
of 1,000 clusters.

00:02:26.590 --> 00:02:29.770
And one interesting aspect
of running hosted Kubernetes

00:02:29.770 --> 00:02:32.140
is that there are versions.

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:35.800
As you know, we move the
master version regularly.

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:37.390
However, the
upgrade of the nodes

00:02:37.390 --> 00:02:40.770
is under your customer control.

00:02:40.770 --> 00:02:42.890
So how do we deal
with that situation,

00:02:42.890 --> 00:02:46.460
because when the gap between
the control plane and the client

00:02:46.460 --> 00:02:50.630
grows, you get you might
run into incompatibility.

00:02:50.630 --> 00:02:53.600
One solution here is to have
node [? out ?] upgrades.

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:55.070
And we have such a feature.

00:02:55.070 --> 00:02:58.820
However, rarely, or not
as often as we would like,

00:02:58.820 --> 00:03:00.260
people use it.

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:01.460
And why is that?

00:03:01.460 --> 00:03:04.360
Because it's hard
to give control up.

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:08.730
And that is because upgrades
bring workload disruption.

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:11.100
And while we try to manage
that workload disruption,

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:12.420
that is the reality of it.

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:15.840
Upgrades bring disruption.

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:19.720
And even though our fleet is
different in shape and sizes,

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:23.540
it's even harder to upgrade
smaller clusters, smaller node

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:26.490
pools, because you don't
have extra capacity that can

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:29.180
cover the disruption period.

00:03:29.180 --> 00:03:30.930
So what actually happens
behind the scenes

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:33.570
when you upgrade a cluster?

00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:36.300
Node by node, we drain a node.

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:38.190
We recreate a VM.

00:03:38.190 --> 00:03:42.600
We wait for that VM to
start to become running.

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:46.530
And then we wait for the node
to be registered with the master

00:03:46.530 --> 00:03:47.590
and become ready.

00:03:47.590 --> 00:03:50.580
And then the
workload is resumed.

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:54.390
So this is the period
where your business might

00:03:54.390 --> 00:03:56.440
experience some disruption.

00:03:56.440 --> 00:03:58.170
How can we make this better?

00:03:58.170 --> 00:04:00.790
Well, if you look
at that timeline,

00:04:00.790 --> 00:04:03.510
you can see that there are
certain things that we can move

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:06.510
out from the rescheduling part.

00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:09.540
And specifically, we
can create an extra VM,

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:14.280
also called surge VM,
before draining the node.

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:17.279
And you can see how disruption
period actually shortens and it

00:04:17.279 --> 00:04:20.399
equals now only the
time that it requires

00:04:20.399 --> 00:04:23.860
Kubernetes to reschedule your
workload from node to node.

00:04:23.860 --> 00:04:25.800
We call this feature
surge upgrades.

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:28.080
We're going to launch it
in alpha this quarter.

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:29.640
You're welcome to use it.

00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:34.030
But what exactly it means
for your workload, well,

00:04:34.030 --> 00:04:37.090
I cannot look inside of your
application but what I can do

00:04:37.090 --> 00:04:40.960
is that I can create a simple
demo app that is a little bit

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:43.450
more sophisticated
than a stateless thing.

00:04:43.450 --> 00:04:45.250
There is a state here.

00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:47.680
There is a storage that
I read and write into.

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:50.560
On each of the
graphs, the upper line

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:52.690
is the queries per
second or request

00:04:52.690 --> 00:04:55.180
per second for reads and writes.

00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:59.300
And the lower line
is the error rate.

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:02.530
So the upper half of
the graph is actually

00:05:02.530 --> 00:05:05.950
what happens to the workload
when we do the regular upgrades

00:05:05.950 --> 00:05:07.030
as we do them right now.

00:05:07.030 --> 00:05:07.840
Kill a node.

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:09.730
Let it come up.

00:05:09.730 --> 00:05:11.890
You can see that there
are dips in the traffic

00:05:11.890 --> 00:05:14.620
as well as spikes in the errors.

00:05:14.620 --> 00:05:16.450
The lower half of
the screen shows

00:05:16.450 --> 00:05:20.560
you the same app running on a
similar cluster of three nodes

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:22.600
but during the surge upgrades.

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:25.120
You can see that deep
[? search ?] more shallow

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:28.810
and also the errors
are not that frequent.

00:05:28.810 --> 00:05:33.610
But even that proves that
there is only so much magic

00:05:33.610 --> 00:05:35.800
that we can do for you
in order to make sure

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:39.550
that your application
is reliable.

00:05:39.550 --> 00:05:41.980
It's not a silver
bullet solution.

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:44.810
There is still some things
that you can do as a customer.

00:05:44.810 --> 00:05:46.810
And this is where I would
like to take my Google

00:05:46.810 --> 00:05:51.190
hat off and just talk about
what workload disruptions are

00:05:51.190 --> 00:05:55.030
and what you can do to
protect yourselves against it.

00:05:55.030 --> 00:05:57.270
So let's take a look
at my simple app.

00:06:03.050 --> 00:06:06.230
I'm running three nodes, which
is the default size on GKE

00:06:06.230 --> 00:06:07.430
as you know.

00:06:07.430 --> 00:06:08.210
It's not too much.

00:06:08.210 --> 00:06:09.830
It's not too little.

00:06:09.830 --> 00:06:14.060
It's exactly what you need to
run some sufficient workload.

00:06:14.060 --> 00:06:16.910
As you can guess from the
names of those two pods,

00:06:16.910 --> 00:06:18.740
I'm running Redis.

00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:20.730
It's an implementation detail.

00:06:20.730 --> 00:06:23.090
I'm not telling you that
you should run Redis.

00:06:23.090 --> 00:06:26.120
It's just to show you
that there is some state.

00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:28.690
And to make things a little
bit more interesting,

00:06:28.690 --> 00:06:32.210
I actually have a persistent
volume connected to that Redis.

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:38.470
So what happens here?

00:06:38.470 --> 00:06:42.225
The client attempts to
perform writes and reads.

00:06:42.225 --> 00:06:43.600
And let's take a
look at the code

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:47.790
so that I'm not just
telling you something.

00:06:47.790 --> 00:06:50.730
All you need to know is that
I initiate the client that

00:06:50.730 --> 00:06:52.830
have the connection to Redis.

00:06:52.830 --> 00:06:55.860
And then I attempt a write.

00:06:55.860 --> 00:06:58.230
And if it succeeds, I report it.

00:06:58.230 --> 00:07:00.390
If it doesn't, I also report it.

00:07:00.390 --> 00:07:01.677
And then I read.

00:07:01.677 --> 00:07:02.260
And that's it.

00:07:02.260 --> 00:07:06.100
That's all I have in
my simple demo app.

00:07:06.100 --> 00:07:07.530
So let's take a
look at the logs.

00:07:13.340 --> 00:07:16.340
So there are some reads
and writes happening.

00:07:16.340 --> 00:07:19.290
When we upgrade a
node, we take it down.

00:07:19.290 --> 00:07:23.030
And that means that we
actually kill your pod.

00:07:23.030 --> 00:07:23.530
Right.

00:07:33.710 --> 00:07:35.390
And that means that
at a certain point

00:07:35.390 --> 00:07:37.670
your application starts
[? turning ?] errors.

00:07:37.670 --> 00:07:39.170
Of course, Kubernetes
rescheduled

00:07:39.170 --> 00:07:41.150
the port and the
situation recovers.

00:07:41.150 --> 00:07:45.260
However, there is a period
during which you serve errors.

00:07:45.260 --> 00:07:48.830
If we delete the client
itself, as you can guess,

00:07:48.830 --> 00:07:51.140
it's going to be even worse.

00:07:51.140 --> 00:07:53.576
You will stop serving
traffic altogether.

00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:01.950
So you have no connection
to the customer altogether.

00:08:01.950 --> 00:08:04.410
What can you do to amend that?

00:08:04.410 --> 00:08:06.300
Let's not talk about
Redis for a second here.

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:08.100
Let's talk only
about the client.

00:08:08.100 --> 00:08:09.900
One of the obvious
things to do here

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:12.240
is just increase the number
of replicas that you have.

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:17.800
And let's just make
sure that we know what

00:08:17.800 --> 00:08:19.630
is happening behind the scenes.

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:33.190
So we had one.

00:08:33.190 --> 00:08:34.682
Let's say three.

00:08:34.682 --> 00:08:37.750
Three is a good number.

00:08:37.750 --> 00:08:42.730
So we see that now immediately
we have three apps, three pods,

00:08:42.730 --> 00:08:44.540
scheduled and running.

00:08:44.540 --> 00:08:46.585
But what is actually--
where are they running?

00:08:50.410 --> 00:08:53.140
Well, turns out that
they all got scheduled

00:08:53.140 --> 00:08:57.430
on three different nodes--

00:08:57.430 --> 00:08:59.730
on two nodes.

00:08:59.730 --> 00:09:02.120
Two of the nodes where the
three replicas got scheduled

00:09:02.120 --> 00:09:04.830
are the same, which
means that when

00:09:04.830 --> 00:09:07.390
we attempt to upgrade
that particular node,

00:09:07.390 --> 00:09:12.250
you will still lose 2/3
of your serving capacity.

00:09:12.250 --> 00:09:14.322
So that's still not good enough.

00:09:14.322 --> 00:09:15.780
What can we do
[? to amend ?] that?

00:09:19.010 --> 00:09:23.380
What we can do is that we
can tell the scheduler, hey,

00:09:23.380 --> 00:09:25.900
by the way, I know
what I'm doing,

00:09:25.900 --> 00:09:29.590
and try to schedule
those particular replicas

00:09:29.590 --> 00:09:31.780
on different nodes,
which is to say

00:09:31.780 --> 00:09:35.210
we plan to use podAntiAffinity.

00:09:35.210 --> 00:09:36.410
Let's add that.

00:09:57.650 --> 00:09:59.700
And we can see that
immediately the scheduler

00:09:59.700 --> 00:10:03.600
obeyed our request and
rescheduled the pod so that we

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:06.540
have them evenly
distributed across the nodes

00:10:06.540 --> 00:10:10.960
so that we can rely on at least
2/3 of the client's capacity

00:10:10.960 --> 00:10:13.120
always be available
during an upgrade.

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:21.310
So if you don't remember
anything from this talk today,

00:10:21.310 --> 00:10:23.020
just take this back with you.

00:10:23.020 --> 00:10:23.950
Redundancy.

00:10:23.950 --> 00:10:26.038
Redundancy is important.

00:10:26.038 --> 00:10:28.330
There are many things that
we can do for you at Google,

00:10:28.330 --> 00:10:30.040
but you have to take
care of yourself

00:10:30.040 --> 00:10:32.410
by providing yourself
a little bit of slack.

00:10:32.410 --> 00:10:34.240
What specifically it means.

00:10:34.240 --> 00:10:36.940
If you have no idea what
you're doing, that's OK.

00:10:36.940 --> 00:10:39.190
Just remember the
simplest formula possible

00:10:39.190 --> 00:10:41.830
that [? SRE ?] love, N plus one.

00:10:41.830 --> 00:10:44.810
Just give yourself
some breathing room,

00:10:44.810 --> 00:10:47.500
but not only give
it but also use it.

00:10:47.500 --> 00:10:49.030
You can think about
your application

00:10:49.030 --> 00:10:52.840
as different full domains
that you want to separate then

00:10:52.840 --> 00:10:55.450
run separately off each
other so that, if part

00:10:55.450 --> 00:10:57.670
of your application fails,
the other one still can

00:10:57.670 --> 00:11:00.650
survive and serve some traffic.

00:11:00.650 --> 00:11:04.940
However, is that good enough
if we have redundancy?

00:11:04.940 --> 00:11:07.595
Let's take a look.

00:11:07.595 --> 00:11:09.220
So I have prepared
a different cluster,

00:11:09.220 --> 00:11:10.600
which is a little bit more.

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:17.470
And what I have here is
I have many more nodes.

00:11:17.470 --> 00:11:19.660
I have nine nodes in total.

00:11:19.660 --> 00:11:24.160
I also have three replicas of
a client as we saw earlier.

00:11:24.160 --> 00:11:25.210
I added them.

00:11:25.210 --> 00:11:28.540
But here I also
have six replicas

00:11:28.540 --> 00:11:31.810
of Redis, which are
configured to be a cluster.

00:11:31.810 --> 00:11:34.627
If you don't know what a Redis
cluster is, it doesn't matter.

00:11:34.627 --> 00:11:37.210
What you have to think about it
is there is enough redundancy.

00:11:37.210 --> 00:11:39.380
There are three
masters, three slaves,

00:11:39.380 --> 00:11:41.773
and they are connected in
such a way that if you lose--

00:11:41.773 --> 00:11:42.940
there's a certain threshold.

00:11:42.940 --> 00:11:46.610
If you lose certain capacity,
you're still good to go.

00:11:46.610 --> 00:11:48.260
But is that good enough?

00:11:48.260 --> 00:11:48.760
Let's see.

00:11:54.560 --> 00:11:58.240
So let's say I was
working on a script.

00:11:58.240 --> 00:12:00.010
And I just forgot
what I was doing

00:12:00.010 --> 00:12:03.080
so I just want to run it
to see what was happening.

00:12:03.080 --> 00:12:07.500
Turns out I was working on
a script to drain a cluster.

00:12:07.500 --> 00:12:10.010
However I left it midway.

00:12:10.010 --> 00:12:14.630
And as you can see, I actually
caused a total outage.

00:12:14.630 --> 00:12:17.690
I cordoned all the
nodes and I also

00:12:17.690 --> 00:12:20.990
removed all the pods, evicted
all the pods from those nodes.

00:12:20.990 --> 00:12:23.415
Let me quickly restore
that so that we

00:12:23.415 --> 00:12:24.540
can continue with the demo.

00:12:29.080 --> 00:12:32.680
Which means that redundancy
that we had didn't save us.

00:12:32.680 --> 00:12:36.100
In a matter of seconds,
I created a total outage.

00:12:36.100 --> 00:12:42.310
And that can be a human
error or an honest mistake.

00:12:42.310 --> 00:12:44.740
What can you do to protect
yourself against that?

00:12:44.740 --> 00:12:47.230
And again, Kubernetes
is such a huge universe

00:12:47.230 --> 00:12:49.100
that there are many,
many mechanisms.

00:12:49.100 --> 00:12:50.530
But the simplest
thing you can do

00:12:50.530 --> 00:12:55.920
is you can add the
pod disruption budget.

00:12:55.920 --> 00:12:59.710
And pod disruption
budget is a place

00:12:59.710 --> 00:13:04.140
where you say, OK, these are
the pods that I want to protect.

00:13:04.140 --> 00:13:07.080
And this is the rule
that I want to apply

00:13:07.080 --> 00:13:10.200
to the particular
deployment or stateful set

00:13:10.200 --> 00:13:11.730
or whatever you have.

00:13:11.730 --> 00:13:14.970
For instance, max
unavailable can be only one.

00:13:14.970 --> 00:13:18.570
At a time, there should be
only one pod unavailable,

00:13:18.570 --> 00:13:24.580
or similarly, just a show of
what pod disruption budget has,

00:13:24.580 --> 00:13:27.320
you can also specify
min available.

00:13:27.320 --> 00:13:30.790
How many at a time should
be available in [INAUDIBLE]..

00:13:30.790 --> 00:13:31.730
Let's apply those two.

00:13:49.620 --> 00:13:53.690
So we now have
those two in place.

00:13:53.690 --> 00:13:56.360
And we have actually
our application

00:13:56.360 --> 00:13:59.290
restored and running.

00:13:59.290 --> 00:14:02.230
And you can see that
PTB says that there

00:14:02.230 --> 00:14:05.650
is allowed disruption
of pod number one

00:14:05.650 --> 00:14:08.470
for each of those
deployments that I have.

00:14:08.470 --> 00:14:12.880
So what happens if I will try
to just drain everything again?

00:14:15.990 --> 00:14:18.870
Well, this is [? over here. ?]
It looks like good news.

00:14:18.870 --> 00:14:21.690
I have messages coming back
that cannot evict the pod.

00:14:21.690 --> 00:14:24.390
And that's because
the scheduler knows

00:14:24.390 --> 00:14:27.000
that I have certain
rules in place

00:14:27.000 --> 00:14:30.250
as to how many replicas
should be running at a time.

00:14:30.250 --> 00:14:32.130
And you can see
that, yes, I managed

00:14:32.130 --> 00:14:38.515
to get rid of one pod in each
stateful set and deployment,

00:14:38.515 --> 00:14:40.390
and I also managed to
[? cordon ?] the nodes,

00:14:40.390 --> 00:14:41.943
but there is no total outage.

00:14:41.943 --> 00:14:43.110
We're still serving traffic.

00:14:43.110 --> 00:14:45.975
We're still OK for the time
when we try to recover.

00:14:52.380 --> 00:14:53.843
So redundancy is not enough.

00:14:53.843 --> 00:14:55.510
You have to put in
place some protection

00:14:55.510 --> 00:14:56.827
if you are extra paranoid.

00:14:56.827 --> 00:14:58.410
And that's right to
be extra paranoid.

00:15:01.270 --> 00:15:03.430
There will be no demo,
but, additionally, you

00:15:03.430 --> 00:15:07.270
can also help us to help you
by providing a little bit

00:15:07.270 --> 00:15:08.950
more detail of
what exactly you're

00:15:08.950 --> 00:15:10.450
running in your black box.

00:15:10.450 --> 00:15:13.330
And specifically you can
use the readiness probe

00:15:13.330 --> 00:15:15.280
and the liveness
probe, two things that

00:15:15.280 --> 00:15:18.250
are used during the
Kubernetes lifecycle that

00:15:18.250 --> 00:15:21.280
help us understand is your
application ready to receive

00:15:21.280 --> 00:15:22.000
traffic.

00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:23.500
And that's readiness probe.

00:15:23.500 --> 00:15:26.230
Or is your application
healthy enough?

00:15:26.230 --> 00:15:27.520
And that's liveness probe.

00:15:27.520 --> 00:15:29.020
If not, let's restart.

00:15:29.020 --> 00:15:30.745
In case of a Redis
that I was using,

00:15:30.745 --> 00:15:32.620
those particular things
would be just pinging

00:15:32.620 --> 00:15:34.690
the [? live ?] Redis
CLI to make sure

00:15:34.690 --> 00:15:38.960
that the instance is available.

00:15:38.960 --> 00:15:42.010
So with those three
in place, let's take

00:15:42.010 --> 00:15:44.920
a look what happened to
the demo app that I had.

00:15:44.920 --> 00:15:48.160
This is just to remind you
how the graphs look before.

00:15:48.160 --> 00:15:51.210
And this is after
the app was tuned.

00:15:51.210 --> 00:15:53.280
So even with the
current upgrade mode

00:15:53.280 --> 00:15:56.550
that we have when we have
to kill a VM at a time,

00:15:56.550 --> 00:15:59.220
you're still already
doing much better.

00:15:59.220 --> 00:16:01.740
With surge upgrades,
you're almost golden.

00:16:01.740 --> 00:16:03.990
But there will be no
magic in this world,

00:16:03.990 --> 00:16:08.130
so you will have disruption
at any point of upgrades.

00:16:08.130 --> 00:16:13.140
You just have to accept it as
price for staying up to date.

00:16:13.140 --> 00:16:14.700
And just a couple
of words of things

00:16:14.700 --> 00:16:17.060
that you should not be doing.

00:16:17.060 --> 00:16:21.110
One of them is specify
min/maxUnavailable zero

00:16:21.110 --> 00:16:23.450
or mean available equals
to the number of replicas

00:16:23.450 --> 00:16:27.110
that you're running, because
for us it's just a hard stop.

00:16:27.110 --> 00:16:29.900
We cannot do anything, because
the scheduler will refuse

00:16:29.900 --> 00:16:34.120
to drain a node where we have
one of your ports running.

00:16:34.120 --> 00:16:36.730
Similarly, you should be careful
about the liveness probe,

00:16:36.730 --> 00:16:40.240
because if the liveness probe
that you define does not

00:16:40.240 --> 00:16:43.150
sustain a load,
and it will fail,

00:16:43.150 --> 00:16:46.840
that means that Kubernetes will
think that the application is

00:16:46.840 --> 00:16:48.670
unhealthy and
reschedule it, which

00:16:48.670 --> 00:16:51.080
means just cascading
failure if you already

00:16:51.080 --> 00:16:53.973
are experiencing a severe load.

00:16:53.973 --> 00:16:55.890
In the same manner, a
termination grace period

00:16:55.890 --> 00:16:57.210
should be something meaningful.

00:16:57.210 --> 00:17:00.260
It cannot be zero, which means
that we don't wait whatsoever,

00:17:00.260 --> 00:17:05.140
or it cannot be hours when
we have to just wait hours.

00:17:05.140 --> 00:17:08.559
And with that, I would like
to hand over to Justin.

00:17:08.559 --> 00:17:09.809
JUSTIN WATTS: I'm from Loblaw.

00:17:09.809 --> 00:17:13.650
And we are Canada's
largest retailer.

00:17:13.650 --> 00:17:15.790
And it's interesting
to think about that.

00:17:15.790 --> 00:17:17.652
Canada's like the
second largest--

00:17:17.652 --> 00:17:19.319
North America's second
largest landmass.

00:17:19.319 --> 00:17:22.740
There's only 33 million
people that live there.

00:17:22.740 --> 00:17:25.540
And so it's crazy
to think that it's

00:17:25.540 --> 00:17:28.920
like 85% of Canadians shop
in one of our locations

00:17:28.920 --> 00:17:29.620
every week.

00:17:29.620 --> 00:17:30.360
Right.

00:17:30.360 --> 00:17:31.980
And so that's a
whole lot of trust

00:17:31.980 --> 00:17:33.730
that our country places in us.

00:17:33.730 --> 00:17:36.070
And it's a really deep
customer relationship.

00:17:36.070 --> 00:17:38.940
And so as a result, our
mission is pretty big.

00:17:38.940 --> 00:17:42.790
We want to help
Canadians live life well.

00:17:42.790 --> 00:17:45.030
And so for me at
Loblaw Digital, my goal

00:17:45.030 --> 00:17:47.700
is to do that in the
lens of cloud native.

00:17:47.700 --> 00:17:49.650
And so I'm really
humbled to work and run

00:17:49.650 --> 00:17:52.140
a team called
engineering productivity.

00:17:52.140 --> 00:17:55.380
And for us, our mission is
to reduce toil, increase

00:17:55.380 --> 00:17:57.370
happiness, and get shit done.

00:17:57.370 --> 00:17:57.870
Right.

00:17:57.870 --> 00:18:00.480
And it's the
confluence of trying

00:18:00.480 --> 00:18:04.020
to have the philosophies of
DevOps and of SRE and of shift

00:18:04.020 --> 00:18:08.460
left, and thinking about how
we enable engineers to solve

00:18:08.460 --> 00:18:10.470
problems rather
than hiring people

00:18:10.470 --> 00:18:13.260
to verify if engineers
solve those problems.

00:18:13.260 --> 00:18:13.830
Right.

00:18:13.830 --> 00:18:15.600
It's all about enablement.

00:18:15.600 --> 00:18:19.050
And what we really love is
that in Kubernetes we're

00:18:19.050 --> 00:18:21.515
able to bring that
conversation together.

00:18:21.515 --> 00:18:22.890
And the great
demos that you just

00:18:22.890 --> 00:18:25.830
saw that manifest YAML,
whether it's actually

00:18:25.830 --> 00:18:29.730
how we deploy the application,
scale the application,

00:18:29.730 --> 00:18:31.753
adding liveliness
and readiness checks,

00:18:31.753 --> 00:18:33.420
you would have SREs
[? entering ?] that.

00:18:33.420 --> 00:18:35.400
You would have DevOps
engineers editing that.

00:18:35.400 --> 00:18:37.368
Heck, you should
have QAs or anyone

00:18:37.368 --> 00:18:38.910
in your organization
actually looking

00:18:38.910 --> 00:18:40.170
at understanding what that is.

00:18:40.170 --> 00:18:41.820
That's your source of truth.

00:18:41.820 --> 00:18:42.510
Right.

00:18:42.510 --> 00:18:44.460
Everything is code.

00:18:44.460 --> 00:18:46.830
And so overall, the
fundamental goal we have

00:18:46.830 --> 00:18:50.340
is to operate at the
intersection of innovation

00:18:50.340 --> 00:18:51.630
and stability.

00:18:51.630 --> 00:18:53.960
And what's funny is I was
thinking about this today.

00:18:53.960 --> 00:18:56.490
Loblaw's actually a
100-year-old company.

00:18:56.490 --> 00:18:58.920
And I think about what
we're doing in Kubernetes

00:18:58.920 --> 00:19:01.310
and thinking about managing
load and resiliency.

00:19:01.310 --> 00:19:03.060
And there's this really
interesting tidbit

00:19:03.060 --> 00:19:04.530
I know from our Wikipedia page.

00:19:04.530 --> 00:19:07.020
We actually invented
the shopping aisle

00:19:07.020 --> 00:19:09.690
in the late 1800s.

00:19:09.690 --> 00:19:12.000
And before then, if
you look at old movies,

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:13.560
there was a dude
behind the counter

00:19:13.560 --> 00:19:15.090
and he would grab your
stuff and give it to you.

00:19:15.090 --> 00:19:15.630
Right.

00:19:15.630 --> 00:19:17.130
Like the max number
of customers you

00:19:17.130 --> 00:19:21.420
could serve as one, a
redundancy factor of one.

00:19:21.420 --> 00:19:24.150
If he didn't make it to
work, nothing could happen.

00:19:24.150 --> 00:19:27.980
And the shopping idea is
it's that innovation of what

00:19:27.980 --> 00:19:29.730
if we could actually
serve more customers,

00:19:29.730 --> 00:19:32.460
and they could serve themselves
and make their lives better.

00:19:32.460 --> 00:19:34.680
And it's interesting
being in retail

00:19:34.680 --> 00:19:38.470
where we have the ability to
impact not only technology

00:19:38.470 --> 00:19:41.160
but what's happening in the
real world with our customers.

00:19:41.160 --> 00:19:41.730
Right.

00:19:41.730 --> 00:19:43.380
And it's not always
in the data center

00:19:43.380 --> 00:19:44.670
that those wins are happening.

00:19:44.670 --> 00:19:47.350
It's sometimes actually
in the physical world.

00:19:47.350 --> 00:19:49.710
And so it's just been
really interesting to see

00:19:49.710 --> 00:19:55.500
how that same message
carries on through 100 years.

00:19:55.500 --> 00:19:57.245
So specifically,
what I'd really like

00:19:57.245 --> 00:19:58.620
to talk to you
about today-- it's

00:19:58.620 --> 00:20:01.300
a very thin sliver
of what we do,

00:20:01.300 --> 00:20:04.470
but it's about how GKE has
really made our lives easier.

00:20:04.470 --> 00:20:07.540
In 2018, one thing that was
really great for us was it

00:20:07.540 --> 00:20:09.900
was the first year that we
had a Black Friday and Cyber

00:20:09.900 --> 00:20:12.360
Monday where none of
our systems crashed.

00:20:12.360 --> 00:20:15.570
And even more impressive
for us was that we actually

00:20:15.570 --> 00:20:19.230
maintained our SLA for response
time and error rate throughout.

00:20:19.230 --> 00:20:21.817
And that's just really thanks
to Google and that mission.

00:20:21.817 --> 00:20:23.400
It's the first year
we've been running

00:20:23.400 --> 00:20:25.290
entirely natively in GCP.

00:20:25.290 --> 00:20:27.840
And we're really proud
of that accomplishment.

00:20:27.840 --> 00:20:29.910
And so it's great to
focus on error rate.

00:20:29.910 --> 00:20:32.770
It's great to focus on
response rate and just be up.

00:20:32.770 --> 00:20:34.380
But for me and my
team, like I said,

00:20:34.380 --> 00:20:35.667
it's engineering productivity.

00:20:35.667 --> 00:20:37.250
It's about getting
things done faster.

00:20:37.250 --> 00:20:38.300
It's about reducing toil.

00:20:38.300 --> 00:20:40.290
It's about increasing happiness.

00:20:40.290 --> 00:20:42.780
And something that's
really, really [? toilful ?]

00:20:42.780 --> 00:20:43.790
is upgrades.

00:20:43.790 --> 00:20:44.460
Right.

00:20:44.460 --> 00:20:46.002
Coming from Canada's
largest retailer

00:20:46.002 --> 00:20:49.200
running I believe the largest
data center in the country--

00:20:49.200 --> 00:20:51.780
we have two of them,
a PR and a DR--

00:20:51.780 --> 00:20:52.910
upgrades are a nightmare.

00:20:52.910 --> 00:20:53.410
Right.

00:20:53.410 --> 00:20:55.890
They take an entire team of
people, a very concerted effort

00:20:55.890 --> 00:20:57.932
to think about how are we
keeping our packages up

00:20:57.932 --> 00:21:01.290
to date, our operating
systems up to date,

00:21:01.290 --> 00:21:02.850
and our servers up to date.

00:21:02.850 --> 00:21:04.710
And so when we saw
the ability to use

00:21:04.710 --> 00:21:06.660
the functionality
of auto upgrades,

00:21:06.660 --> 00:21:08.490
we were there,
because we didn't want

00:21:08.490 --> 00:21:11.850
that to have to be a burden
that our team took on.

00:21:11.850 --> 00:21:14.160
And so auto upgrades have
three main benefits--

00:21:14.160 --> 00:21:17.730
security, reliability,
and functionality.

00:21:17.730 --> 00:21:20.280
And a great example,
if you all know

00:21:20.280 --> 00:21:22.820
the runC violation
that recently came out,

00:21:22.820 --> 00:21:24.990
it was a really serious CV.

00:21:24.990 --> 00:21:27.720
By the time we knew about
it, we were already safe.

00:21:27.720 --> 00:21:28.220
Right.

00:21:28.220 --> 00:21:29.678
And that's thanks
to the great work

00:21:29.678 --> 00:21:33.290
that the Google team are
doing to keep the masters--

00:21:33.290 --> 00:21:35.520
and actually in that
case, it was container

00:21:35.520 --> 00:21:38.250
OS didn't even have the flaw.

00:21:38.250 --> 00:21:39.250
And there's another one.

00:21:39.250 --> 00:21:40.800
There was a privilege
escalation CV

00:21:40.800 --> 00:21:42.360
that happened late last year.

00:21:42.360 --> 00:21:45.210
And that was actually having
those auto upgrades on,

00:21:45.210 --> 00:21:48.750
before we found out about
it, we were already safe.

00:21:48.750 --> 00:21:50.830
And so from a
security perspective,

00:21:50.830 --> 00:21:52.230
it seems like a no brainer.

00:21:52.230 --> 00:21:54.522
But something that Kate did
a great job of highlighting

00:21:54.522 --> 00:21:58.770
is that work disruption can
be a really serious issue.

00:21:58.770 --> 00:22:00.570
And so we love automation.

00:22:00.570 --> 00:22:02.310
We love magic.

00:22:02.310 --> 00:22:06.560
But sometimes later
can be safer than now.

00:22:06.560 --> 00:22:09.500
And I'll give you a
good example as to why.

00:22:09.500 --> 00:22:14.690
If I'm in retail, when I upgrade
is actually really important.

00:22:14.690 --> 00:22:16.790
If we are in the
middle of Black Friday

00:22:16.790 --> 00:22:19.370
or if it's even a
Thursday, because Thursday

00:22:19.370 --> 00:22:21.110
is when we launch
our promotions,

00:22:21.110 --> 00:22:23.690
I probably don't want to
run that risk of having

00:22:23.690 --> 00:22:26.520
a disruption happen,
because in the best case,

00:22:26.520 --> 00:22:29.090
we get the really nice
graph that Kate showed.

00:22:29.090 --> 00:22:32.690
In the worst case, that
graph's a whole lot nastier.

00:22:32.690 --> 00:22:36.020
And so today opting into
auto upgrades, I feel great.

00:22:36.020 --> 00:22:37.460
Toil's reduced.

00:22:37.460 --> 00:22:39.740
But I don't have
quite enough control.

00:22:39.740 --> 00:22:43.130
Today Google will allow you to
set a four hour window of when

00:22:43.130 --> 00:22:44.540
they should do their upgrades.

00:22:44.540 --> 00:22:46.332
But there's a couple
of things that doesn't

00:22:46.332 --> 00:22:47.750
account for unfortunately.

00:22:47.750 --> 00:22:49.910
One thing it doesn't
account for is the day

00:22:49.910 --> 00:22:51.680
of the week to do that upgrade.

00:22:51.680 --> 00:22:53.557
And for that that's
important to us.

00:22:53.557 --> 00:22:55.890
And the second is the calendar
year like thinking ahead.

00:22:55.890 --> 00:22:58.210
We know when our
big milestones are.

00:22:58.210 --> 00:23:00.560
And for retail obviously,
once again, Black Friday

00:23:00.560 --> 00:23:02.690
and Cyber Monday, those
two are going to come up.

00:23:02.690 --> 00:23:04.670
Please don't touch
our infrastructure

00:23:04.670 --> 00:23:07.220
around these times,
or if you're working

00:23:07.220 --> 00:23:09.950
in a company that has
code freezes for holidays

00:23:09.950 --> 00:23:12.380
or vacations where you
don't have staff on hand,

00:23:12.380 --> 00:23:13.980
that's really important.

00:23:13.980 --> 00:23:16.190
So some of you might not be
opting into auto upgrades

00:23:16.190 --> 00:23:18.225
because you don't
have that control.

00:23:18.225 --> 00:23:19.100
We've taken the risk.

00:23:19.100 --> 00:23:21.140
And for us, reducing the
toil of having to do it

00:23:21.140 --> 00:23:24.020
ourselves was worth the
potential risk of it

00:23:24.020 --> 00:23:28.350
going wrong and us having to
be there at an [? odd ?] time.

00:23:28.350 --> 00:23:31.820
And then the other one is
about how you upgrade them.

00:23:31.820 --> 00:23:34.160
Right now it's an all
or nothing approach.

00:23:34.160 --> 00:23:35.870
And when we do it--

00:23:35.870 --> 00:23:38.510
Google does it
node by node today.

00:23:38.510 --> 00:23:40.460
And what we've
noticed is that we're

00:23:40.460 --> 00:23:43.970
running a multi-tenant,
multi-cluster,

00:23:43.970 --> 00:23:46.230
and a multi-regional
cluster at that.

00:23:46.230 --> 00:23:48.260
At Loblaw, one
interesting thing is

00:23:48.260 --> 00:23:50.812
we believe that all of our
businesses are good neighbors.

00:23:50.812 --> 00:23:52.520
And that allows us to
invest in something

00:23:52.520 --> 00:23:55.373
we call soft multi-tenancy.

00:23:55.373 --> 00:23:57.790
We don't have to have the
strict isolation because there's

00:23:57.790 --> 00:23:59.930
an implicit trust
between, let's say,

00:23:59.930 --> 00:24:02.360
Joe Fresh, which is
our retailing clothing

00:24:02.360 --> 00:24:04.910
brand and Shoppers Drug
Mart, where we're doing

00:24:04.910 --> 00:24:07.033
beauty products and pharmacy.

00:24:07.033 --> 00:24:08.450
And we actually
might want to make

00:24:08.450 --> 00:24:11.075
that a really strong connection,
because they may have services

00:24:11.075 --> 00:24:13.520
that talk to each other like
their shared communal log in,

00:24:13.520 --> 00:24:16.340
which is our PCI-D application.

00:24:16.340 --> 00:24:18.940
And so for us multi-cluster
has been really important.

00:24:18.940 --> 00:24:21.920
But when we do an upgrade,
when we're exposing that auto

00:24:21.920 --> 00:24:23.780
upgrade and that's
going node by node,

00:24:23.780 --> 00:24:26.270
we're actually impacting
a lot of our businesses.

00:24:26.270 --> 00:24:28.020
And so one feature
that we've really loved

00:24:28.020 --> 00:24:30.050
is this idea of node pulls.

00:24:30.050 --> 00:24:32.000
And node pulls you
can actually have it

00:24:32.000 --> 00:24:35.400
so that an upgrade happens
in a specific node pool.

00:24:35.400 --> 00:24:38.480
And so we definitely recommend
not just using the default node

00:24:38.480 --> 00:24:38.980
pool.

00:24:38.980 --> 00:24:39.480
Right.

00:24:39.480 --> 00:24:41.840
And whether you're t-shirt
sizing small, medium,

00:24:41.840 --> 00:24:44.240
or large for certain
workloads or doing it

00:24:44.240 --> 00:24:46.520
per business, that
gives you an ability

00:24:46.520 --> 00:24:48.380
to break up your
upgrades and make sure

00:24:48.380 --> 00:24:52.110
they're safe in one
place before moving on.

00:24:52.110 --> 00:24:57.240
The second thing that we've seen
is the what are we upgrading.

00:24:57.240 --> 00:24:57.740
Right.

00:24:57.740 --> 00:24:59.210
And for those of
you today-- like

00:24:59.210 --> 00:25:02.150
there's just a lot about
Kubernetes versions.

00:25:02.150 --> 00:25:04.670
It's moving extremely quickly.

00:25:04.670 --> 00:25:06.710
And whether it's patch,
whether it's major,

00:25:06.710 --> 00:25:09.300
or whether it's minor, for
us when we're auto upgrading,

00:25:09.300 --> 00:25:12.560
we feel good about that we're
getting new security updates,

00:25:12.560 --> 00:25:16.212
but we're not quite sure
what else is coming.

00:25:16.212 --> 00:25:18.170
And so I feel like I'm
sleeping safer at night,

00:25:18.170 --> 00:25:19.920
but I don't know what
to tell my engineers

00:25:19.920 --> 00:25:21.380
they now have access to.

00:25:21.380 --> 00:25:23.360
And we felt like
we didn't really

00:25:23.360 --> 00:25:27.920
have enough control at times to
understand what channel we're

00:25:27.920 --> 00:25:29.660
even on, like that
bleeding edge of alpha

00:25:29.660 --> 00:25:31.415
versus beta versus stable.

00:25:31.415 --> 00:25:33.290
And so we're hoping that
we can see some more

00:25:33.290 --> 00:25:34.950
control in those areas.

00:25:34.950 --> 00:25:37.710
But at the same time from
a productivity perspective,

00:25:37.710 --> 00:25:42.180
we're really thankful that
we can upgrade very quickly.

00:25:42.180 --> 00:25:47.210
And so overall, later can
sometimes be safer than now.

00:25:47.210 --> 00:25:50.360
I would suggest that everyone
cares about these upgrades

00:25:50.360 --> 00:25:53.780
and their own sanity get off
the manual train into auto

00:25:53.780 --> 00:25:55.790
by doing so get the
feedback to Google

00:25:55.790 --> 00:25:59.360
for how automatic upgrades
can be made better.

00:25:59.360 --> 00:26:03.550
And I think overall we
will be in a better place

00:26:03.550 --> 00:26:05.260
if we're focused on engineering.

00:26:05.260 --> 00:26:08.170
As a retailer, we want
to sell groceries.

00:26:08.170 --> 00:26:11.530
I really don't want to be in the
business of managing operating

00:26:11.530 --> 00:26:14.050
systems, managing
upgrades, and having

00:26:14.050 --> 00:26:15.250
to staff people for that.

00:26:15.250 --> 00:26:16.830
And at the end of the day,
the people that we staff

00:26:16.830 --> 00:26:19.570
to do that, I don't know if
anyone was born with the idea

00:26:19.570 --> 00:26:23.350
that my big dream in life is
to upgrade operating systems.

00:26:23.350 --> 00:26:27.510
Like operations engineers, we
have big ideas and big dreams.

00:26:27.510 --> 00:26:29.260
And when I look about
the amazing features

00:26:29.260 --> 00:26:31.780
that Kate's showing off, that's
what we want our engineers

00:26:31.780 --> 00:26:32.300
to be doing.

00:26:32.300 --> 00:26:33.412
Right.

00:26:33.412 --> 00:26:34.870
And so I think we
should aim higher

00:26:34.870 --> 00:26:37.060
in that regard for the
type of work we're doing

00:26:37.060 --> 00:26:40.360
and the impact that we're
having in our organizations.

00:26:40.360 --> 00:26:44.770
So overall, we're really
excited by these new features

00:26:44.770 --> 00:26:45.980
that are coming out.

00:26:45.980 --> 00:26:49.420
We're heavily invested in this
from the Loblaw digital side.

00:26:49.420 --> 00:26:52.967
And I'll hand it back to Kobi
to see what's coming next.

00:26:52.967 --> 00:26:54.300
KOBI MAGNEZI: Thank you, Justin.

00:26:58.450 --> 00:27:00.850
So so far we spoke
about challenges.

00:27:00.850 --> 00:27:04.190
We saw some amazing demos.

00:27:04.190 --> 00:27:06.880
And we heard from Justin
about their experience.

00:27:06.880 --> 00:27:08.950
Next I'm excited to
share with you some

00:27:08.950 --> 00:27:10.903
of the new capabilities
we are working on

00:27:10.903 --> 00:27:12.820
to actually provide you
with even more control

00:27:12.820 --> 00:27:14.720
and flexibility.

00:27:14.720 --> 00:27:16.630
We'll be focusing
on three areas--

00:27:16.630 --> 00:27:21.050
reduce risk, control
disruption, and manage risk.

00:27:21.050 --> 00:27:24.080
Let's talk about the first
one, how you can reduce risk.

00:27:24.080 --> 00:27:26.360
And Kate mentioned it before.

00:27:26.360 --> 00:27:30.380
So we are actually changing the
way we will be upgrading nodes

00:27:30.380 --> 00:27:33.320
in clusters managed by GKE.

00:27:33.320 --> 00:27:35.060
Right now the way
we upgrade nodes

00:27:35.060 --> 00:27:37.400
is at the time of
the upgrade we'll

00:27:37.400 --> 00:27:39.260
go one node after the other.

00:27:39.260 --> 00:27:42.830
And we'll remove that
node and bring a new one

00:27:42.830 --> 00:27:44.420
with the new version.

00:27:44.420 --> 00:27:47.370
That's obviously slightly
disruptive for customers.

00:27:47.370 --> 00:27:50.340
And we are changing that
process with the surge upgrade.

00:27:50.340 --> 00:27:52.100
So with surge upgrade,
we'll actually

00:27:52.100 --> 00:27:54.380
create a new node first.

00:27:54.380 --> 00:27:58.630
And then we'll gracefully
drain the old one.

00:27:58.630 --> 00:28:03.080
And at that time, we'll
remove the old node.

00:28:03.080 --> 00:28:05.310
What that gives you
is a few things.

00:28:05.310 --> 00:28:08.270
One, it allows us to secure
resources before we even

00:28:08.270 --> 00:28:12.560
start upgrades, because we
create that node before we

00:28:12.560 --> 00:28:14.790
take the old one down.

00:28:14.790 --> 00:28:17.480
The second thing is we
ensure successful completion

00:28:17.480 --> 00:28:18.860
of the node upgrade.

00:28:18.860 --> 00:28:21.720
We protect your capacity.

00:28:21.720 --> 00:28:22.220
Right.

00:28:22.220 --> 00:28:23.630
So at any given
point of time you

00:28:23.630 --> 00:28:25.610
have the number of
nodes you expect

00:28:25.610 --> 00:28:27.860
to have even during upgrades.

00:28:27.860 --> 00:28:30.590
And that in turn reduce
disruption and reduce downtime.

00:28:33.990 --> 00:28:38.590
Justin mentioned the Maintenance
Windows support we have today.

00:28:38.590 --> 00:28:41.670
So yes, you can create
Maintenance Windows.

00:28:41.670 --> 00:28:42.810
And we will respect that.

00:28:42.810 --> 00:28:45.060
Basically, the feature
that we have right now

00:28:45.060 --> 00:28:49.570
supports four hours Maintenance
Windows in any given day.

00:28:49.570 --> 00:28:52.800
So obviously there is less
flexibility right now.

00:28:52.800 --> 00:28:55.380
Next we are actually going
to enhance the maintenance

00:28:55.380 --> 00:28:57.900
windows' capabilities to
support different use cases,

00:28:57.900 --> 00:29:00.660
like the use case
that Justin mentioned.

00:29:00.660 --> 00:29:04.867
So let's say you prefer to
avoid upgrades during weekends,

00:29:04.867 --> 00:29:07.200
because you are a gaming
company and most of the traffic

00:29:07.200 --> 00:29:10.320
is coming in through
the weekends.

00:29:10.320 --> 00:29:14.220
You'll be able to do that
with the enhanced maintenance

00:29:14.220 --> 00:29:15.840
windows support.

00:29:15.840 --> 00:29:17.880
If you are a
retailer and you are

00:29:17.880 --> 00:29:21.810
trying to avoid completely any
maintenance events during Black

00:29:21.810 --> 00:29:23.250
Friday, Cyber
Monday, you will be

00:29:23.250 --> 00:29:25.020
able to specify
the range of dates

00:29:25.020 --> 00:29:26.880
and we will respect
that and avoid upgrades

00:29:26.880 --> 00:29:28.590
during those times.

00:29:28.590 --> 00:29:31.550
And then lastly, you know
about upcoming upgrade

00:29:31.550 --> 00:29:33.210
but you just need more time.

00:29:33.210 --> 00:29:34.890
You need more time
to prepare or you

00:29:34.890 --> 00:29:37.830
have some internal challenges
that you're facing.

00:29:37.830 --> 00:29:40.440
You'll be able to
postpone or snooze

00:29:40.440 --> 00:29:43.560
an upgrade for a
short period of time.

00:29:43.560 --> 00:29:48.400
That gives you more flexibility
in controlling disruption.

00:29:48.400 --> 00:29:49.930
The other way to
control disruption

00:29:49.930 --> 00:29:51.730
is really our release notes.

00:29:51.730 --> 00:29:54.340
And transparency is
really key for us.

00:29:54.340 --> 00:29:57.080
In the release notes, you get
much more information than just

00:29:57.080 --> 00:29:59.020
a change log.

00:29:59.020 --> 00:30:02.790
You'll get information about
the upcoming release schedule,

00:30:02.790 --> 00:30:05.310
the releases that
we are deprecating,

00:30:05.310 --> 00:30:08.110
the releases that we are
going to make available.

00:30:08.110 --> 00:30:11.440
And the other thing is we
have a four days roll out

00:30:11.440 --> 00:30:13.570
policy in GKE.

00:30:13.570 --> 00:30:16.680
So we roll out new version
in different regions

00:30:16.680 --> 00:30:18.140
during four days.

00:30:18.140 --> 00:30:20.840
You can actually see that
schedule in the release notes.

00:30:20.840 --> 00:30:23.020
So we are publishing
what version

00:30:23.020 --> 00:30:25.270
is coming first in which
region and you can actually

00:30:25.270 --> 00:30:29.000
plan your upgrade accordingly.

00:30:29.000 --> 00:30:30.460
Let's talk about managing risks.

00:30:30.460 --> 00:30:32.000
We spoke about ways
to manage risk.

00:30:32.000 --> 00:30:34.000
And I think PDP or
pod disruption budget

00:30:34.000 --> 00:30:38.590
is super important capability
that we support today,

00:30:38.590 --> 00:30:41.270
especially if you have
a stateful application.

00:30:41.270 --> 00:30:44.560
But in order to support
your risk mitigation,

00:30:44.560 --> 00:30:48.010
canary cluster will allow
you to create a cluster

00:30:48.010 --> 00:30:51.970
and designate it for canary
or staging environment.

00:30:51.970 --> 00:30:54.910
That in turn will indicate
to us that we should roll out

00:30:54.910 --> 00:30:56.950
new versions to
those canary cluster

00:30:56.950 --> 00:30:59.690
before we roll them out to
your production clusters.

00:30:59.690 --> 00:31:02.830
So there will be a mapping
between your canary clusters

00:31:02.830 --> 00:31:04.480
and your production clusters.

00:31:04.480 --> 00:31:05.820
So we'll roll out new version.

00:31:05.820 --> 00:31:08.020
We'll give you enough time
to run your automate this

00:31:08.020 --> 00:31:10.040
to get some confidence.

00:31:10.040 --> 00:31:12.370
And when you green
light, the diversion

00:31:12.370 --> 00:31:18.090
we'll continue rolling out the
upgrades to production as well.

00:31:18.090 --> 00:31:23.320
And another exciting feature
in Q2 is release channels.

00:31:23.320 --> 00:31:26.560
We spoke about the amount
of versions we support.

00:31:26.560 --> 00:31:28.990
And it's really overwhelming.

00:31:28.990 --> 00:31:31.600
And we are trying to make
it easier for customer

00:31:31.600 --> 00:31:34.870
and move away from that
structure of supporting

00:31:34.870 --> 00:31:38.610
many, many versions to
supporting three channels.

00:31:38.610 --> 00:31:42.170
We are thinking right now about
rapid, regular, and stable.

00:31:42.170 --> 00:31:44.680
So if you want to get
early access to the latest

00:31:44.680 --> 00:31:48.580
and greatest bleeding edge
version of Kubernetes,

00:31:48.580 --> 00:31:50.600
you probably want to be
on the rapid channel.

00:31:50.600 --> 00:31:52.250
And let's say you have
a testing environment.

00:31:52.250 --> 00:31:54.625
You will be able to say, hey,
this is my testing clusters

00:31:54.625 --> 00:31:58.270
and I want to subscribe this
cluster to the rapid channel.

00:31:58.270 --> 00:32:02.390
We'll make sure to roll
new bleeding edge versions

00:32:02.390 --> 00:32:04.360
in that specific cluster.

00:32:04.360 --> 00:32:06.130
Same thing with
regular and stable.

00:32:06.130 --> 00:32:08.380
Regular will give you
the default version

00:32:08.380 --> 00:32:10.750
like the one we have today
available in the fleet.

00:32:10.750 --> 00:32:13.900
And stable you will
probably get less features,

00:32:13.900 --> 00:32:17.590
but it will be more stable
in terms of the version

00:32:17.590 --> 00:32:21.790
accumulate more soak time and
we have more test coverage

00:32:21.790 --> 00:32:25.660
for that specific channel.

00:32:25.660 --> 00:32:27.910
Just a quick example
of how this may look

00:32:27.910 --> 00:32:30.130
like from a command
line perspective.

00:32:30.130 --> 00:32:33.830
So you can create a new
cluster and subscribe it

00:32:33.830 --> 00:32:35.470
to the rapid channel.

00:32:38.340 --> 00:32:41.880
So we spoke about different
and many great features.

00:32:41.880 --> 00:32:44.310
And I just want to take a
moment to kind of bring it

00:32:44.310 --> 00:32:45.670
all together.

00:32:45.670 --> 00:32:48.390
Our goal is to keep your
cluster up and running

00:32:48.390 --> 00:32:52.350
and deliver non-disruptive
experience even

00:32:52.350 --> 00:32:53.730
during upgrades.

00:32:53.730 --> 00:32:57.023
And visibility is key for
us as I mentioned before.

00:32:57.023 --> 00:32:59.190
We want to make sure that
you understand our product

00:32:59.190 --> 00:33:02.430
lifecycle and you
can plan accordingly.

00:33:02.430 --> 00:33:05.400
We aim to provide hassle
free managed Kubernetes

00:33:05.400 --> 00:33:08.190
service so you can focus
on what you care about

00:33:08.190 --> 00:33:10.350
and your core business.

00:33:10.350 --> 00:33:12.690
If you are interested in
any one of the features

00:33:12.690 --> 00:33:15.510
that I mentioned today, please
follow this link and you

00:33:15.510 --> 00:33:18.540
can sign up and share
with us your interest

00:33:18.540 --> 00:33:22.050
in joining the alpha program
for one of these features.

00:33:22.050 --> 00:33:25.400
[MUSIC PLAYING]

